  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nCanadian high pressure will continue to provide tranquil weather\nconditions today. Mainly sunny skies this morning will becoming\npartly cloudy this afternoon as some weak daytime instability\ndevelops, resulting in a fair weather cu field. High\ntemperatures will be near seasonable averages, with readings\nranging from the lower 70s across the north to the upper 70s and\nlower 80s south. \n\nMainly clear skies are expected tonight, which combined with\nlight winds and relatively low dew points will allow for\nradiational cooling. Forecast lows range from the lower to\nmiddle 40s across some interior valleys with upper 40s and lower\n50s elsewhere. Valley fog is likely to develop overnight and\npersist into early Sunday morning. \n\nSunday begins with high pressure overhead, quickly exiting east\ninto the open Atlantic. Surface return flow combined with a WSW\nlow level flow will begin to saturate the low levels while high\nclouds overrun from the west. Right now, mid level saturation \nisn't likely until the late afternoon or evening, and this keeps\nrain chances lower through the day until the evening and \novernight hours.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nForecast ensemble IVT plumes differ in magnitude and \ntrajectory, with a more northeastward segment from the ECMWF ENS\nthan the GEFS (a northward suppression, more progressive \nthrough the Mid Atlantic). In line with this vapor transport in \nthe ENS, EFI QPF fields have also jumped comparing guidance from\nWednesday evening to Thursday evening's runs indicating some \npotential for an above average precipitation event. Shift of \ntails however remains muted, with an extreme precipitation event\nunlikely. \n\nWarm cloud depth in deterministic models is impressive, with \nruns of the GFS, ECMWF, NAM, and Canadian all depicting depths \ngreater than 12kft for a period late Sunday night and early \nMonday morning. Coinciding with above normal PWATs around 2 \ninches, a period of heavy rain is becoming more likely, but \nexact location and radar presentation will need to be resolved \nwith time.\n\nOne of the larger differences in guidance, and therefore an\nimportant distinction between outcomes, is how far south the\ntrough or low cuts into southern New England. A track further\ninto southern New England could kink vapor transport, limiting\nmoisture depth and duration, while a flatter path across\nnorthern New England keeps this SW moisture flux open, resulting\nin more efficient rain processes.\n\nRemaining rain tapers off through the day Monday, with drier\nweather and a bit of a warmup through midweek as highs climb\ninto the lower to mid 80s. The next chance for rain then looks\nto be Wednesday into Thursday as an elongated cold front\napproaches from Quebec. It is yet to be determined if a wave\ndevelops out of this front, but could offer some rain\nenhancement depending on location through northern New England.",
